%% SECTION Scope and Quality
@@
Which technique is most appropriate when users have difficulty describing how they currently perform a complex process?
A) Observation (job shadowing)
B) Questionnaires
C) Benchmarking
D) Voting
= A
? Observation reveals hidden or tacit requirements when people struggle to articulate how they work.

@@
A requirements workshop brings together users, developers and testers from different departments to define requirements quickly and resolve conflicts. In software development, this is often called:
A) Joint application design (JAD)
B) Quality function deployment only
C) A bidder conference
D) A retrospective
= A
? JAD sessions are facilitated workshops for software requirements. QFD (voice of the customer) is a similar technique used in manufacturing.

@@
Which statement about the WBS is correct?
A) It is a deliverable-oriented hierarchical decomposition of the total scope of work
B) It lists all project activities in the chronological order in which they will be performed
C) It shows the reporting relationships between the project team and functional departments
D) It describes only the work that external contractors will perform under the contract
= A
? The WBS decomposes scope into deliverables and work packages. Sequencing is done in the schedule; reporting lines are shown in an OBS.

@@
A control account manager compares the budget, schedule and earned value for a group of work packages. What is a control account?
A) A management point where scope, budget and schedule are integrated and measured
B) A dedicated bank account that is used to receive and disburse all of the project's funds
C) The lowest level of the WBS, where activities are defined and sequenced
D) A summary of the risks that could affect a group of related work packages
= A
? Control accounts integrate scope, budget and schedule and contain one or more work packages.

@@
The customer reviews the completed training materials and signs an acceptance form. Which output has been produced?
A) Accepted deliverables
B) Verified deliverables
C) Quality control measurements
D) Work performance data
= A
? Formal sign-off by the customer produces accepted deliverables in Validate Scope.

@@
Which document should be checked to determine whether a requested feature is out of scope?
A) The project scope statement, especially the exclusions
B) The communications management plan and its distribution list
C) The resource calendar for the team members involved
D) The team charter and its decision-making guidelines
= A
? The scope statement defines deliverables and exclusions; it clarifies whether the request is within scope.

@@
A product owner uses the categories Must have, Should have, Could have and Won't have this time. Which technique is this?
A) MoSCoW prioritisation
B) Kano analysis
C) Monte Carlo simulation
D) Pareto analysis
= A
? MoSCoW classifies requirements by priority for a timebox or release.

@@ | multi
Which two statements correctly describe the definition of done? (Choose two.)
A) It applies to every backlog item the team completes
B) It is a shared understanding of the quality criteria for an increment
C) It is written separately by the customer for each user story
D) It replaces acceptance criteria entirely
E) It is only reviewed at project closure
= A, B
? The DoD is a team-wide quality standard applied to all work. Acceptance criteria remain specific to each story.

@@
During testing, the team finds that a deliverable does not meet the specified tolerance. Which process detects this?
A) Monitor and Control Scope (quality control)
B) Validate Scope (customer acceptance review)
C) Manage Quality Assurance (process audit)
D) Elicit and Analyze Requirements (analysis)
= A
? Inspection and testing of deliverables against requirements is quality control, part of Monitor and Control Scope in the Eighth Edition.

@@
A manufacturing project spends money on inspecting incoming materials. Which cost of quality category is this?
A) Appraisal
B) Prevention
C) Internal failure
D) External failure
= A
? Inspection and testing are appraisal costs incurred to assess quality.

@@
A process's control chart shows one point above the upper control limit. What does this mean?
A) The process is out of control and the special cause should be investigated
B) The process is in control because only a single point is outside the limit
C) The specification limits should be adjusted to include the new measurement
D) The control limits were calculated wrongly and must be recalculated at once
= A
? A single point outside the control limits indicates a special cause of variation that should be investigated.

@@
A Pareto analysis shows that 3 of 15 defect types cause 78% of customer complaints. What should the team do?
A) Focus improvement efforts on those three defect types first
B) Address all fifteen defect types equally so that none is neglected
C) Start with the least frequent defect types because they are quickest to fix
D) Collect another year of data before deciding where to focus improvements
= A
? The Pareto principle directs effort to the vital few causes that create most of the problems.

@@
Which quality tool is used to tally occurrences of defects by type during inspections?
A) Check sheet
B) Scatter diagram
C) Flowchart
D) Control chart
= A
? Check sheets organise data collection by category, and their results are often used to build Pareto diagrams.

@@
What does the requirements traceability matrix help a project manager do when a stakeholder requests a change to a requirement?
A) Identify the deliverables, designs and tests affected by the change
B) Calculate the cost performance index of the affected work packages
C) Determine which team members are available to implement the change
D) Decide which contract type should be used for the changed requirement
= A
? Traceability links requirements forward to deliverables and tests, supporting impact analysis.

@@
A team wants to verify whether training hours correlate with lower defect rates. Which tool fits best?
A) Scatter diagram
B) Histogram
C) Fishbone diagram
D) Pareto chart
= A
? Scatter diagrams plot two variables to identify correlation.

@@
Which statement about Validate Scope is correct?
A) It is performed periodically with the customer or sponsor, usually after quality control
B) It is performed only once, at the very end of the project, during the final closing meeting
C) It is another name for quality control and is carried out by the testing team
D) It is performed by the project team alone, without involving the customer
= A
? Validate Scope is repeated for deliverables over time; it obtains formal acceptance after deliverables have been verified.

@@
A team member wants to add an extra report "because it would be nice to have". The scope baseline does not include it. What is the best response?
A) Explain that it would be gold plating and suggest raising it with the product owner
B) Approve it, since a more complete set of reports always improves product quality
C) Let the team member add it quietly, since small extras help to impress the customer
D) Add the report and remove a required report so that the total effort stays the same
= A
? Gold plating adds cost and risk without agreed value. If the idea has merit, it goes through the appropriate channel.

@@
Which document describes, for each WBS element, the work, responsible organisation, milestones, cost estimates and acceptance criteria?
A) WBS dictionary
B) Requirements documentation
C) Project charter
D) Quality report
= A
? The WBS dictionary provides detailed information for each WBS element and is part of the scope baseline.

@@
In the Kano model, a hotel guest expects hot water in the shower and is very dissatisfied without it, but having it does not increase satisfaction. This feature is:
A) A basic (must-be) quality
B) An excitement (delighter) feature
C) A performance (linear) feature
D) An indifferent feature
= A
? Basic qualities cause dissatisfaction when absent but no extra satisfaction when present.

@@
A product owner asks the team to split a large epic. Which characteristic of good user stories does this mainly support?
A) Small enough to be completed within one iteration
B) Dependent on other stories so they are delivered together
C) Fixed and non-negotiable once written by the product owner
D) Valuable mainly to the developers who will implement it
= A
? INVEST stories are Small enough to fit in an iteration, as well as Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable and Testable.

@@
Which practice best reflects "prevention over inspection"?
A) Training the team and using checklists and automated tests before defects occur
B) Doubling the number of final inspectors so that more defects are caught before shipping
C) Letting customers report defects and then fixing them quickly through support
D) Relaxing the specification limits so fewer units are classified as defective
= A
? Prevention reduces the chance of defects; it is cheaper than finding them through inspection or by customers.

@@ | match
Match each quality concept with its description.
1) Grade
2) Precision
3) Accuracy
4) Tolerance
A) Category assigned to products with the same use but different characteristics
B) Repeated measurements are closely clustered
C) Measured value is close to the true value
D) Acceptable range of variation for a quality metric
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Grade differs from quality; precision is consistency; accuracy is correctness; tolerance is the allowed variation.

@@
In the Eighth Edition, which process includes planning how quality will be achieved, such as defining quality metrics?
A) Define Scope
B) Develop Budget
C) Plan Communications Management
D) Close Project or Phase
= A
? Quality planning was incorporated into Define Scope.

@@
A requirement states: "Customer data must be migrated from the legacy system before go-live." Which requirement type is this?
A) Transition and readiness requirement
B) Non-functional requirement
C) Business requirement
D) Project requirement only
= A
? Transition requirements describe temporary capabilities needed to move from the current to the future state, such as data conversion and training.

@@
A product's process is in statistical control, but many units still fall outside the customer's specifications. What does this indicate?
A) The process is stable but not capable of meeting the specification, so it must be improved
B) The control limits should be widened so that they match the customer's specification limits
C) The customer's specifications are unrealistic and should be changed to match the process
D) The process is out of statistical control and its special causes must be investigated
= A
? Control limits describe the process; specification limits describe customer requirements. A stable process can still fail to meet specifications.
